WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320
We've all seen hundreds of bug bounty reports and we know that bug bounty can look very

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:07.600
easy.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.640
But how does a life of an actual full-time bug bounty hunter look like?

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:19.080
To find this out, I interviewed Justin Gardner, who's one of the best hackers out there participating

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.480
in multiple live hacking events, who became full-time hunter about 4 years ago.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.120
Trust me, you do not want to miss this episode.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.720
Enjoy the interview.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.680
Hello Justin, thank you so much for joining me in today's podcast.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:42.080
For those of you who don't know Justin, he's a host among being a great bug bounty hunter.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:46.000
He's the host of the Critical Thinking Bug Bounty podcast and I want to thank you and

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.080
Joel as well for creating such a fantastic resource.

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:54.400
I think it's literally the best resource about bug bounty at the moment.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.560
So huge thanks for this.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:58.560
That's awesome man.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:00:59.560
Thank you.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:04.640
Yeah of course, it's great to be on the pod man and it's fun actually being on the flip

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.960
side of this scenario, right?

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.160
Normally I'm the one being like, hey thanks for joining me on the pod and carrying the

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.160
conversation.

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:17.720
So I'm just going to sit back today and answer some questions and relax.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:18.720
Should be fun.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:19.720
Yeah, easy.

00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:20.720
Almost easy job.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.820
Actually it will be me just asking questions and listening to you and you'll do all the

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.320
hard work this time.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.600
Well, that's true.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:33.240
Okay, for those viewers who don't know you, they know they have to check Critical Thinking

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:38.120
but after this podcast and for now, can you please talk us through your background?

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.320
How did you start with hacking?

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:42.320
Sure man.

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:49.040
Yeah, so the background for me was, well I'm particularly blessed in that I knew what I

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.040
wanted to do for my job ever since I was like 12 years old.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:00.880
And that all goes back to one day when I was working or I was playing my games when

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:07.000
I was 12 and a guy from my church actually came over when my internet stopped working

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.000
because I was like super, I was in the middle of a game and the internet just dropped and

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.640
I was like, oh this is terrible.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:16.760
And a guy came over and he fixed my internet.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.240
And he did that by typing into a little command line, you know the little black box command

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:27.640
line terminal and then all of a sudden, you know, it worked again.

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:35.080
And he typed like ipconfig slash release ipconfig renew and you know, just reset the lease there

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.680
and it worked.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:41.100
And I was like, whoa, okay, hold on, I got to learn how to do that.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:47.400
So I started Googling around and I started bothering him every week at church.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.480
I'd be like, hey, teach me how to do this, teach me how to do this.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:56.040
And so that was where the interest really started, but that was just IT.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:02.200
So as I started, you know, learning more about computers, I started getting into programming

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:07.200
and as I started getting into programming, I started to get into hacking.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.120
And at that time, it was largely me writing hacking tools in Python because I really liked

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:20.240
Python and I was the moderator of a forum called Hack Forums.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.600
I was of the Python section of that.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.360
And that was really fun.

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:31.600
And so I guess from there, it kind of, my interest sort of grew.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:40.800
I did have my little stint on the dark side of hacking and, you know, went a little bit

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.480
down that path.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:49.640
But after I decided that wasn't where I wanted to be ethically, I switched over to more focusing

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:52.160
on programming for the next couple of years.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.800
And then after that, I went to college and restarted my hacking journey, which is where

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.880
I got acquainted with Bug Bounty.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.280
And the story behind that is really interesting.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:08.200
And I've actually, I think I've talked about this once or twice before, but that journey

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:15.200
actually started for me with a guy named Tommy DeVos, the dog EG, many, many people know

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.640
him in the community.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:23.040
And he just kind of rolled into my cybersecurity club that I was teaching one day and started

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.280
telling me about Bug Bounty.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:26.120
I didn't even know he was going to come.

00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:30.040
And like, I was in the middle of teaching a lecture on how to, like, hack something

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:31.680
on Metasploitable.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.680
And he just rolls in.

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:33.680
He's like, yo, what's up?

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.920
And in the middle of the lecture, and I'm like, hey, how's it going?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.080
He's like, you guys want to pop some of these systems?

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.520
And he's pointing at these live computers.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:42.360
I'm like, okay, who are you?

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.720
You know?

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:45.600
And he proceeded to tell me about Bug Bounty.

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:48.840
And that's kind of when my life changed.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:57.000
And I did a little stint in, like, pen testing consulting, you know, before I jumped straight

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.280
into full-time Bug Bounty.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:04.480
But really, from that moment on, from that moment that Tommy DeVos introduced me to it,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:09.680
I've been really captivated by Bug Bounty.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.360
And one question before we continue.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.160
Where does your nickname come from?

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:16.160
Sure.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:17.160
Yeah, man.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:18.160
I get that question a lot.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.360
And actually, Franz was kind of making fun of me.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:26.520
At the last event, he put an emoji of, like, a person thinking, and then a rhinoceros,

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.680
and then, like, B+, or something like that.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:34.520
Like, I'm rating rhinos, like, on a scale of, you know, F to A+, or whatever.

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:40.360
But actually, the name comes from a video game that I used to play called Ratcheting

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:41.360
Plank.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:45.640
And in that game, there's a gun called the Rhino Blitzerator.

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:50.880
And being the weird 12-year-old that I was when I came up with this, I decided to take

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.960
the only cool part of that name out of it, and remove Blitz from it, and then just stick

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:57.960
with Rhino Rater.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.800
So that's where it came from.

00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:01.760
No correlation at all to rhinoceroses.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.000
Okay.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.000
It was a nice, nice, catchy nickname.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:05.000
Thanks, man.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.840
And the cool story behind it as well.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:13.000
So you worked for a few years as a pen tester.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.920
What were the things that attracted you in bug bounty that you didn't like in pen testing,

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:18.920
maybe?

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:19.920
Yeah, man.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:23.800
Well, I really enjoyed my stint as a penetration tester.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:30.760
I worked for a great company in Wisconsin called Synercom, S-Y-N-E-R-C-O-M-M.

00:06:30.760 --> 00:06:32.120
And they're a great company.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:33.880
I really enjoyed working with them.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:38.720
I enjoyed the management and the whole flow.

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.040
But the thing that really pulled me away was the flexibility.

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:46.720
Like at the end of the day, if you're not working for yourself, you'll never have complete

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:47.720
flexibility.

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:48.720
And that's what I wanted.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:56.080
So when Mariah and I were getting ready to move to Japan, which we did in 2020, I forced

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:02.000
myself to move out of my comfort zone a little bit and leave that job and go full-time bug

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:05.960
bounty because the financials were where they needed to be for that.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.920
And I was definitely a little anxious about it at the time.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.200
But with Mariah's support, we were able to do it.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:18.360
How did you prepare to quit a nine-to-five job?

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:22.240
Well, you know, I'm pretty risk-averse as a person.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.400
I'm kind of growing a little bit more away from that now.

00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:31.760
But I was really not attracted to the idea of not having a consistent income.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.680
And so for me, it looked like saving up a lot of money to make sure I had enough runway.

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.520
So essentially, Mariah and I said, OK, we're going to save up.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.680
We're going to make sure that we have enough money to live in Japan for a year without

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:46.160
making any money at all off of bug bounty.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:49.840
But at that point, I was already making more money off of bug bounty than I was making

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.960
at my full-time job.

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:57.120
And so, you know, it made sense that if I made the switch, I would be making at least

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.280
that much, if not more.

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:06.320
So that sort of confidence, you know, running those numbers, getting that runway in place,

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.360
that was a good thing for me.

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:16.680
And a lot of people mentioned the unpredictable income as one of the biggest cons of being

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:18.680
a full-time bug bounty hunter.

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:20.840
How do you manage this now?

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:25.040
Yeah, well, now it's a little bit more, I'm a little bit more comfortable with the whole

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:26.040
situation.

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:30.560
So I don't manage it as strictly as I did before.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.520
But one of the best ways to manage this, and I talked about this on the last podcast that

00:08:34.520 --> 00:08:41.800
we did at Critical Thinking with Renny Pak, is you can take some money, put it in a business

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.800
account, right?

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:47.240
Do bug bounty part-time until you build up, you know, however much you need in your business

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:48.240
account.

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:54.360
And then just decide, all right, I'm going to pay myself out, you know, 5K, 10K, 15K

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:59.320
a month, whatever it is, from this account to my bank account, just like I had a normal

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:00.960
job, you know?

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.320
And if you have enough runway in there in advance, if you've got, you know, let's say

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:11.560
if it's 5K, if you've got, you know, 30 grand, $30,000 in there, then you'll, you know, you've

00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:15.400
got six months of runway of paying yourself without ever having to earn any money.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.720
And that should be pretty stabilizing, I think.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.480
Yeah, yeah, I did a similar thing.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:27.200
When I was quitting my job, the way I thought about it is I have one year of worth of living

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:33.760
in my savings, and I treated every single, let's say, every single thousand was a few

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.880
days longer that I didn't have to come back to employment.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:38.880
Exactly, exactly.

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:44.320
And then you pop bugs like that 20K S3 bucket thing, and then you extend that runway a lot.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:45.760
Yeah, it extends a lot.

00:09:45.760 --> 00:09:50.240
I don't even know how long it is now, but at least I do feel comfortable.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.840
That's good.

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:57.680
And one more thing about the full-time bank bounty.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.640
What are the things that make you reconsider your decision and make you want to be just

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:05.520
employed again and don't care about a lot of things?

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:11.400
Well, dude, you're asking me this question at a pretty, like, rough time, actually, for

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.400
me, because I've been sitting and thinking for the past couple days.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:21.640
I'm coming off of a trip to Japan and off a trip to Portugal for some life hacking events,

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:24.920
and I'm just kind of now getting back into my swing.

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:29.440
And actually, when you asked me to prepare for this podcast, you asked me to grab some

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:33.120
stats on my bug bounty reports and that sort of thing.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:39.440
And while my income is on track this year, I'm realizing that I'm not really a full-time

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:41.360
bug bounty hunter this year.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:49.080
This year, I'm a full-time live hacking event participant is essentially what's happening.

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:55.880
Looking back over my reports, they are very much largely clustered around the live hacking

00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:56.880
events.

00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:01.440
And then after that, I take a break, or as I mentioned on the pod before, I do real estate

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:02.440
stuff on the side.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:07.680
So this year in January, we bought a rental property, and I've been fixing that up, going

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:14.000
there, hammering stuff and drilling stuff and that sort of thing, and we just finally

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:15.000
finished that.

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:23.520
So I haven't been quite on top of it this year, and that affects the confidence a little.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:28.480
I'm still doing all right in the live hacking events, but when you're not staying in the

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:32.960
requests every single day, and you're not getting as much time, and I think it's also

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:37.520
coupled with the fact that I've been doing critical thinking, which takes time.

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:43.240
I'm just not as in the request as I used to be.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:47.360
And so that's something that I'm taking steps to fix, but in the meantime, it feels a little

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:54.080
bit like that feeling starts to creep back a little bit, that familiar feeling of, man,

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.000
is this really the right choice for me?

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.200
And I know I'm ranting here, but I actually do have one other thing.

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:07.200
Starting full-time book bounty, I think it's really tricky to be self-employed and to rest

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:14.200
properly, because I find it really hard for me to say, all right, six o'clock, and then

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:19.360
I'm done, if I've got stuff that I need to do.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:25.480
And so yesterday, it was a beautiful fall day, and I got the hot tub in the backyard,

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:31.120
and I'm like, okay, I'm just gonna grab a drink, be done with work, grab a drink, Mariah's

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.200
coming with me, we go sit out in the hot tub, drink the drink, look at the beautiful

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.000
fall colors, and I'm sitting there, and I'm like, man, this is great, but why am I still

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:44.960
feeling a little anxiousness in my chest, you know?

00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:46.920
I know it so well.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.080
It's bad, man, and so it takes a lot of mental effort, and a lot of mental, and so I sat

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:59.760
there in that moment, and I said, hey, breathe, like, you have enough time to do the work

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:05.000
that you need to do, you have enough income to survive, why are you stressing out?

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:06.920
There's no reason, right?

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:13.800
And so sometimes you just gotta take a breath, breathe through it, and meditate on that fact

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:20.880
that everything is fine, and you don't need to worry about all these little details.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:24.560
And I didn't have that problem before when I was employed, traditionally, when I had

00:13:24.560 --> 00:13:28.360
a consulting job, because at the end of the day, I'd clock out, and I'd be like, well,

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:30.520
this is somebody else's problem now, you know?

00:13:30.520 --> 00:13:36.480
So it's definitely different, and those things like that do make me question the decision

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.720
a little bit every once in a while.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:44.440
Yeah, it's difficult to relax without, to like fully relax.

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:50.800
For me, I think sports is great, because then I'm literally so into whatever activity I

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.040
am doing that I can't even think this.

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.040
Yeah, yeah.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:56.160
But it's definitely difficult.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:57.880
I play volleyball every week.

00:13:58.400 --> 00:14:01.320
I was playing volleyball until midnight last night, and it's great.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:07.960
But for me, that's not rest.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:14.600
It's mental rest, but sometimes not even mental rest, because it's like there's a lot of strategy

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:20.480
that goes into volleyball, and I'm a very competitive person, and so when I'm playing

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:23.680
volleyball, I'm in the zone.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:30.000
I think trying to figure out how to have strict boundaries with yourself, and actually, here,

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.560
let me show you one other trick.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:38.360
For those of you listening on, I'm sorry, but I'm going to show something on the screen

00:14:38.360 --> 00:14:39.360
here for YouTube.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:44.760
This is a sign that my wife got me, and this says in Japanese, open for business, and this

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:51.280
one says closed, or planned days off, essentially.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:58.240
And when I'm working, I flip the sign to in business, and when I'm done working, I flip

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:05.160
the sign to it's rest time, and that does help me sort of mentally segregate, all right,

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:11.280
I need to be done with work now, and I need to stop letting it invade my thoughts and

00:15:11.280 --> 00:15:12.760
invade my stress.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:21.240
Yeah, it's so smart, because being self-employed just creates a lot of problems like this,

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.840
that you are not even thinking about when you're quitting.

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:29.320
When you're quitting, you're thinking about, will I make enough money, I will be able to

