boop, blocked it down, bummer.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:45.960
Yeah, okay, let's get back to hacking.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:50.200
Attacking both is nice, but how do you attack web applications, what's your hacking style?

00:17:50.200 --> 00:17:58.200
Yeah, you know, I find this question really hard to answer, because it's a pretty abstract

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:03.400
question, and I kind of put some thought into it, and I think the biggest thing for me is

00:18:03.400 --> 00:18:07.880
actually understanding the application, and understanding how users use the application.

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:18.280
So first thing I do when I get into an application is I get excited, and then I think I'm about

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:21.960
to start hacking stuff, and I start looking at the request, and sometimes I'll like read

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:26.920
the JavaScript files right off the bat, and then I almost always catch myself about five

00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:33.160
minutes in, and I'm like, okay, let's actually not try to hack something that we don't

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:34.280
understand.

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:43.160
And I go back, and I minimize my Kydo tab, or my birth tab, or whatever, and send it

00:18:43.160 --> 00:18:47.240
down to the bottom, and where I can't look at it, and then I just use the application

00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:51.080
like a user, and I try to set through all the functionality in the application, I try

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:55.560
to configure everything, sometimes that means going and creating accounts on different services,

00:18:55.560 --> 00:19:01.480
and linking those accounts to this service, and importing data, or filling out spreadsheets

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:04.680
to populate data in the application, whatever it is.

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:10.920
I take the time to do that for a little while, and then I go back, and I start looking through

00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:16.600
the request in Burp, and I start working back through those same things, but with the window

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:22.280
open, and that's when the bugs start rolling, but I think it's important first to use the

00:19:22.280 --> 00:19:29.400
application like a human, and not necessarily just like somebody who's focused on hacking

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.400
it.

00:19:30.480 --> 00:19:34.000
How much time does this phase last, more or less?

00:19:34.000 --> 00:19:39.680
You know, normally about, normally less than an hour, I'd say.

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:47.280
You know, it depends on the size of the application, but normally less than an hour, and then also,

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:53.280
I won't necessarily do this while I'm hacking, but I'll try to, but sometimes I will, but

00:19:53.280 --> 00:19:56.960
I often try to read the documentation too, and that's a great thing to do, like, you

00:19:56.960 --> 00:20:02.400
know, if you have to go somewhere, and you know, your wife is driving or something, and

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:06.400
you can just read the documentation on your phone, or like you're waiting at a doctor's

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:12.880
appointment or something like that, and you know, you can just read the documentation,

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:16.640
and so that's when I'm feeling a little bit, or maybe I'm like burnt out on hacking, my

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:24.480
eyes don't want to look at the screen anymore, so I, you know, throw the, you know, documentation

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:29.280
on my e-reader, my e-ink reader, and then go sit in the hot tub and read the documentation,

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:36.560
you know, and so it works great for getting to know the application a little bit better,

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:42.880
and as we say on the podcast, getting intimate with the application, you know, and I think

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:44.000
that's really important.

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:46.800
I'm not much of a wide hacker.

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:48.960
I don't do a lot of recon.

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:52.240
I mostly just go to the main app and then hack it, sort of person.

00:20:53.200 --> 00:20:57.200
Do you take notes when you're in the hot tub, for example?

00:20:59.920 --> 00:21:00.960
I don't, I don't.

00:21:01.600 --> 00:21:08.160
What I will do sometimes is I will record a voice memo, and then, you know, just kind

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:11.840
of record, and then pause, and then go back to the documentation, record, pause, go back

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:16.080
to the documentation, that sort of thing, and then afterwards, I'll either have that

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:21.760
transcribed by AI, or I'll transcribe it myself, or sometimes I'll send it to Mariah, and she'll

00:21:21.760 --> 00:21:29.200
transcribe it for me, and that does help, but normally, while I'm hacking, my notes

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:35.760
are nothing more than naming my Kaido tabs or something like that, so yeah.

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:42.560
And it's a very difficult question, because you probably rely on your experience a lot,

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:48.080
but what are you looking for in this first phase when you're assessing the application?

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:50.000
Yeah, man, that's a good question.

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:59.040
Yeah, you hit the nail on the head when you said relying on experience, and I think what's

00:21:59.040 --> 00:22:02.480
really important for a lot of people is building that experience, right?

00:22:02.480 --> 00:22:07.040
And the only way you build that experience is by failing, and so get out there, fail,

00:22:07.040 --> 00:22:11.920
look at the applications, but for me, I'm looking for anything that has an ID in the

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:12.640
request, right?

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:17.760
So I can try IDORS, I'm looking for data that's being reflected in the response.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:24.000
Specifically, in older applications, you can just kind of feel it when they reflect

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.640
data into a JavaScript block.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:33.680
One of the things I see a lot is a query parameter or a post parameter gets put inside a variable,

00:22:33.680 --> 00:22:36.640
so in JavaScript, in the response, right?

00:22:36.640 --> 00:22:38.000
And that's just sketchy as heck.

00:22:39.680 --> 00:22:47.200
And so whenever you have those sort of situations, definitely look for XSS.

00:22:47.200 --> 00:22:50.320
I'm looking for places where URLs are being parsed.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:52.960
That's not great stuff.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:57.920
I'm looking for any tokens or signed things, right?

00:22:57.920 --> 00:23:01.280
So I can see if I can manipulate any of that, trying to break out of that syntax.

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:04.640
What else am I looking for?

00:23:04.640 --> 00:23:11.440
Looking for any XML parsing or really any structured language parsing or any interactions

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:16.480
with SDKs or other logical entities.

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:22.400
If they're using a different website to do XYZ, there's got to be sort of an auth transition

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:23.760
between those two things.

00:23:23.760 --> 00:23:24.960
And that's kind of where I'm looking.

00:23:26.560 --> 00:23:31.440
And Dan, what are your favorite bug types that you go for most often?

00:23:32.160 --> 00:23:33.120
Favorite bug types.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.920
Well, lately, I've been loving postMessage stuff.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:38.560
The postMessage stuff is really cool.

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:43.440
And there's a lot of untapped attack surface there.

00:23:44.800 --> 00:23:45.680
So I really like that.

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:55.520
To be honest, man, I think my favorite bugs are the ones that are irregular.

00:23:55.520 --> 00:24:00.400
You know, the ones that are not your IDOR, your SSRF, your RCE, your straightforward,

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.280
you know, just like command injection or anything like that.

00:24:03.280 --> 00:24:07.920
It's something that, like, all right, I, like, inject it into this token, and this token,

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.240
like, gives me half auth into this other app.

00:24:10.240 --> 00:24:14.400
And then, like, with that half auth, I found, like, this one little weird endpoint in the

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.040
API that I can hit that just, like, leaks everything.

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:23.200
You know, that sort of stuff is a lot more engaging or a lot more exciting to me now

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:25.120
than, like, ah, look at this.

00:24:25.120 --> 00:24:27.680
I found an IDOR, you know, sort of thing.

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:28.640
Yeah.

00:24:29.760 --> 00:24:35.280
And I know that you are probably the most famous user of Kaido.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:38.400
What sort of...

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.520
Do you spend all the time in Kaido, or do you use both Verb and Kaido?

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:42.000
What does it look like?

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.600
Yeah, I use both right now.

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:51.840
I would like to switch fully to Kaido, and I use Kaido normally until I need to use Verb.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:57.920
But there are still a couple features missing with Kaido, and I think a lot of this will

00:24:57.920 --> 00:25:03.280
be solved when Kaido implements upstream proxies, which is coming out very soon.

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:09.600
I think you released a podcast, like, two hours ago when we record this about Kaido.

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.720
Yeah, so there's lots of good data in there about Kaido.

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:16.480
But yeah, they're going to be implementing upstream proxies soon, which will allow you

00:25:16.480 --> 00:25:20.320
to chain Kaido with Verb and that sort of thing, which you can already do with Verb

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:21.120
upstream proxies.

00:25:21.120 --> 00:25:25.920
But, you know, I prefer to use Kaido as the main one.

00:25:25.920 --> 00:25:33.040
And then I'm using Verb primarily now for things like quick collaborator callbacks if

00:25:33.040 --> 00:25:34.640
I need it, but really rarely that.

00:25:35.600 --> 00:25:39.600
And then for ParamMiner and some of the plugins.

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:46.000
That's the main thing that I think I'm missing in Kaido is the plugin functionality that

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:46.560
Verb has.

00:25:47.680 --> 00:25:52.960
There are a couple other nice-to-haves, but at the end of the day, I'm working to transition

00:25:52.960 --> 00:26:00.400
my flow from Verb into Kaido and trying to grow with that product because I'm 100% certain

00:26:00.400 --> 00:26:02.800
that it will be taking over the market.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:10.640
Yeah, I think there's a lot of room for a different proxy right now because Verb has

00:26:10.640 --> 00:26:16.240
so many functionalities and it's so great in many aspects, but it's also bad in many

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:16.800
aspects.

00:26:16.800 --> 00:26:19.440
And there's room for competition.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:25.840
And I think one way or another, when we have competition, we as users, we are going to

00:26:28.320 --> 00:26:31.840
win when they fight.

00:26:32.400 --> 00:26:35.120
Fight for the audience.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:36.000
100%, man.

00:26:36.000 --> 00:26:37.600
Yeah, I totally agree.

00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:39.760
And no disrespect to Portswiger.

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:43.200
I talk about Kaido all the time, but Portswiger is a great company.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:48.640
They do so much cool stuff at the Portswiger Academy, and I love the Portswiger Research

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.640
Twitter channel and that sort of thing.

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:54.960
I feel like there are some things we've been asking for with Verb for a while for them

00:26:55.040 --> 00:27:02.400
to solve the structural issues and how much computing power is required to run it.

00:27:02.400 --> 00:27:03.920
And it's just not happening.

00:27:03.920 --> 00:27:09.280
So there's definitely a space for someone else to move into the market, even if it's

00:27:09.280 --> 00:27:12.720
just in the capacity of a lightweight HTTP proxy, right?

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:15.680
It doesn't necessarily have to be everything.

00:27:15.680 --> 00:27:18.160
A lot of hackers, that's all they need.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:22.640
A repeater is the main functionality, so something can work.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:23.200
Absolutely.

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:28.480
What are some Verb extensions that you use most commonly?

00:27:30.640 --> 00:27:33.040
Verb extensions, param minor.

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:34.480
Param minor is my go-to one.

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:35.200
Let me just hold on.

00:27:35.200 --> 00:27:37.600
Let me just open it up here and see what I've got.

00:27:43.520 --> 00:27:45.120
Yeah, so param minor.

00:27:45.840 --> 00:27:56.880
I actually use copy as Python requests pretty often, just to pop it out into a Python file.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:02.800
I use this great plugin called Request Minimizer, which is super helpful.

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:08.160
And essentially what it does is you just click one button, and it sends the request a bunch

00:28:08.160 --> 00:28:17.920
of times, and then it tells you which cookies, which headers, whatever, are necessary for you

00:28:21.040 --> 00:28:22.400
to get the same response.

00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:27.760
And that's really helpful in allowing you to identify what is actually doing something

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:29.920
in the situation, right?

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:31.360
What cookies are being used for auth?

00:28:31.360 --> 00:28:32.800
What headers are being used for auth?

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:38.320
You know, what the CSRF restrictions are actually being implemented, that sort of thing.

00:28:38.320 --> 00:28:39.920
So I use that one.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:41.760
I've got JS Weasel on here.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:51.040
Autorepeater is really cool too, if you're working on an app that does a lot of auth

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:51.920
testing.

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:53.040
So that one's really cool as well.

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:55.120
What does autorepeater do?

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:57.360
Yeah, autorepeater is really cool.

00:28:58.880 --> 00:29:02.160
It essentially allows you to automatically repeat requests.

00:29:03.280 --> 00:29:10.320
And so essentially what it does is you define a set of replacements.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:15.680
For example, if you want the cookies from another account subbed into the request, then

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:19.520
it can do that, and it'll automatically sub, like, CSRF headers and that sort of thing

00:29:19.520 --> 00:29:20.800
if you set it up to do that.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:22.720
And then it'll repeat the request.

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:29.360
And the reason why this is really cool is you can automate, I guess, authorization testing.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:34.400
And so, you know, I just set up, all right, here are the cookies for my second account.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:38.720
Here's a CSRF token that matches the cookies in the second account.

00:29:38.720 --> 00:29:45.520
And every request that I do, it'll sub the cookies, sub the CSRF token in, and tell me

00:29:45.520 --> 00:29:48.880
whether I get a 403 or a 200 and log the response.

00:29:49.680 --> 00:29:55.120
And it has a nice, you know, red-green sort of indicator to determine whether the request

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:55.920
was successful or not.

00:29:55.920 --> 00:30:00.880
So if you're dealing with an app that has a lot of problems with authorization, then

00:30:00.880 --> 00:30:05.200
this can really speed up your testing methodology, because you won't have to send it to repeater,

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:08.080
sub the cookies, sub the CSRF token every time.

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:09.360
It'll just be done automatically.

00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:16.320
It sounds, like, very similar to Authorize extension.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.320
Did you test both of them, or...

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:18.880
Let me see.

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:22.000
Yep, that's the one I'm talking about.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:24.320
I'm talking about Authorize.

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:28.080
I have both of them on next to it, next to each other here.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:29.280
But yeah, that's the one that...

00:30:30.080 --> 00:30:31.520
I'm actually talking about Authorize.

00:30:33.200 --> 00:30:33.520
Yeah.

00:30:33.520 --> 00:30:34.320
Okay, yeah, yeah.

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:35.440
Authorize is great.

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:36.240
I also use it.

00:30:39.920 --> 00:30:46.240
You also said something about not reading JS files in the initial phase of testing the

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:52.400
app, but when the time comes, eventually, to read the JS files, what are your tips to

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:53.840
get the most out of them?

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:58.880
Yeah, so, JS files are really, really important.

00:31:00.080 --> 00:31:05.120
They are one of the top ways, I think, to get intimate with an application.

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:13.120
And so, in order to do that effectively, there's a couple really good tips, and JS

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:15.200
Weasel is implementing a lot of them.

00:31:15.920 --> 00:31:22.160
It's a really expensive software, and there are, once again, features missing from it

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:27.920
that have prevented me from integrating it into my workflow, like, full-fledged.

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:33.360
But once it does complete those features, for example, a global search and a little

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:36.640
bit more clean UI, I think it'll be huge.

00:31:36.640 --> 00:31:41.680
And the reason for that is because so many applications nowadays are using a webpack

00:31:41.680 --> 00:31:46.560
to sort of encapsulate their JS files, right?

00:31:46.640 --> 00:31:54.000
And you need to write a script to take the map for that, which you can find in one of

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:58.960
the files, and build out all the sub-JS files that are lazy-loaded, and get all those as

00:31:58.960 --> 00:31:59.680
well.

00:31:59.680 --> 00:32:03.360
And then once you do, you can find a bunch of really cool endpoints in those.

00:32:04.400 --> 00:32:06.320
And that's really the goal of it all, right?

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:11.840
Is to look at the JavaScript files, look at the functionality that's there, or maybe used

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:18.880
to be there, if it's commented out, and try to see if those endpoints exist on the server

00:32:18.880 --> 00:32:21.840
side, and whether you can recreate that functionality.

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:28.560
And there's lots of different tricks to that, but the primary ones that I can think of now

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:37.200
are look for, take a request that you see going through Kaido or through Burp, and send

00:32:37.280 --> 00:32:43.360
it to, you know, send it to repeater, look at the URL, and try to find the JS file or

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:46.400
the JS code that initialized that request.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:54.480
And then essentially look at the format, look at how that HTTP request is being issued,

00:32:54.480 --> 00:33:00.320
maybe it's fetch, maybe it's, you know, AJAX, maybe it's whatever, and then search for that

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:05.680
same functionality, for that same code, in all the JS files, to see if you can enumerate

00:33:05.680 --> 00:33:09.040
all of the ways that requests are being sent in the application.

00:33:10.640 --> 00:33:13.920
And do you usually do this inside the browser?

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:16.720
Like, let's say, without the JS Weasel?

00:33:16.720 --> 00:33:17.520
Yeah, I do.

00:33:17.520 --> 00:33:19.120
Yeah, I almost always do.

00:33:19.120 --> 00:33:23.840
And there's some really big benefits of that, namely that you can set breakpoints and do

00:33:23.840 --> 00:33:25.120
dynamic analysis as well.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:30.800
But yeah, I would like to do it in JS Weasel, because it's nice to have some of the VS code

00:33:30.800 --> 00:33:31.520
functionality.

00:33:32.240 --> 00:33:34.800
But like I said, for me, it's just not quite there yet.

00:33:34.800 --> 00:33:37.360
I could see it getting there within the next six months, for sure.

