For right now, I don't think I'm going to be able to integrate it into my workflow, but

00:33:54.640 --> 00:33:56.400
implement these features.

00:33:56.400 --> 00:34:00.560
And then if you can give me another trial, I would strongly consider buying this.

00:34:01.680 --> 00:34:05.680
And you get a lot of other big names in the industry, Shubbz, CDL.

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:09.680
These guys are already using it, I believe, on a regular basis.

00:34:09.680 --> 00:34:13.120
So it's definitely a product to keep your eye on and something that very well might be

00:34:13.120 --> 00:34:14.480
worth $1,000 a year.

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:16.720
Yeah, OK.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:24.400
So let's say you did all these things and three, four, five, six, seven, the whole day

00:34:24.400 --> 00:34:26.320
passes, you didn't find the bug.

00:34:27.280 --> 00:34:30.480
What's the moment when you say enough and you move on to another one?

00:34:31.920 --> 00:34:38.240
Dude, I'm not the guy to ask this question, too, because I will not move on until I find

00:34:38.240 --> 00:34:38.640
a bug.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:43.760
And then I will start grasping at straws until I find something.

00:34:45.040 --> 00:34:49.920
In one of the latest live hacking events that I've been to, there's this particularly

00:34:51.680 --> 00:34:54.160
featureless application that we were supposed to hack.

00:34:54.160 --> 00:34:58.080
And everyone was like, yeah, this is really not much here.

00:34:58.640 --> 00:35:02.960
And I just could not let it go until I found something.

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:04.560
And then I found some crazy...

00:35:06.080 --> 00:35:10.400
I won't give the details, but it was very much grasping at straws in the beginning,

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:11.840
but turned into a pretty decent bug.

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:18.000
But yeah, for me, I think really it would have to be two weeks if I wasn't finding...

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:23.520
I really do not like to let applications defeat me.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:28.240
So I would keep on digging as long as the bounties were there.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:34.800
I'm not going to dive two weeks on a 5k crit program, but as long as the bounties are there,

00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:45.120
1k for medium, something for a high, and then above 5k, ideally 10k or above for a crit,

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:46.880
then I'll definitely spend some time on that.

00:35:47.440 --> 00:35:54.640
You also said in one podcast that when you started to spend more time on one application,

00:35:54.640 --> 00:35:58.560
you started finding fewer bugs, but more critical bugs.

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:00.480
Yeah, yeah.

00:36:02.320 --> 00:36:03.360
That's definitely true.

00:36:03.360 --> 00:36:13.360
I think that if you look at my reports from 2021 or 2020,

00:36:13.600 --> 00:36:18.880
or 2020, a lot of my reports were volume.

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:26.560
Find every single IDOR, find every single XSS, that sort of thing, right?

00:36:27.440 --> 00:36:36.640
But then as I started to look at it less as a grind of checking every single endpoint,

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:41.840
every single parameter, and looking at it more holistically from an application perspective,

00:36:41.840 --> 00:36:45.040
and saying, okay, where is the valuable data in this application?

00:36:45.040 --> 00:36:47.600
What are the core pieces of functionality?

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:51.840
What are the fringe pieces of functionality that affect core pieces of functionality,

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:54.560
like the developer API or something like that, right?

00:36:56.240 --> 00:37:02.560
That's when I started finding more complex, more intriguing, and higher impact bugs,

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:03.760
but less of them, for sure.

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:10.640
Yeah, but overall, you're probably happy with finding fewer critical bugs.

00:37:10.640 --> 00:37:11.600
Yeah, yeah, I am.

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:16.640
And sometimes it doesn't work out that way, and sometimes you spend your time,

00:37:16.640 --> 00:37:21.120
and you just find mediums, and lows, and maybe a high or two,

00:37:21.120 --> 00:37:25.680
and that's sometimes how it goes.

00:37:25.680 --> 00:37:30.560
But yeah, I'm always shooting for the higher impact,

00:37:31.120 --> 00:37:34.240
and trying not to shy away from the complex topics as well.

00:37:34.480 --> 00:37:40.640
That's another tip, is like, if you read something in the documentation,

00:37:40.640 --> 00:37:44.320
or you see something in an app, and it looks complex, and it looks difficult to hack,

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:44.800
guess what?

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:46.640
Every other hacker also thought that.

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:52.640
And I bet you that the people that did the pen testing for that application also thought that,

00:37:52.640 --> 00:37:56.640
and said, ah, you know, my 40 hours on this app are better spent elsewhere.

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.040
And there are often bugs there.

00:37:59.200 --> 00:38:04.400
The reason why I placed fourth in the last Integrity event was,

00:38:04.960 --> 00:38:11.440
I focused on a more complex part of the scope,

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:15.840
and was able to land two bugs there that paid out really well.

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:17.200
So definitely a good tip there.

00:38:18.720 --> 00:38:27.600
Okay, so for example, how many programs did you hack for at least a few days in the last,

00:38:27.600 --> 00:38:32.160
let's say, a month, excluding the period now where you prepared for the live hacking event?

00:38:32.720 --> 00:38:35.280
Yeah, so let's take a look at that.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:36.560
I've got some data up here.

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:45.280
So I did some hacking on BARD, and that was really fun.

00:38:45.280 --> 00:38:51.840
And I did some live hacking event prep, and then I did one other program.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:57.280
And that's, yeah, that's in the last month.

00:38:57.520 --> 00:38:58.560
About three programs.

00:38:59.840 --> 00:39:01.760
And I guess...

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:03.760
That's not a lot.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:05.440
Sometimes I go through three in one day.

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:06.160
Yeah, yeah.

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:07.840
Well, I would never do that.

00:39:08.880 --> 00:39:14.240
Like, I have to spend at least a couple days on a program.

00:39:14.240 --> 00:39:18.560
And for me, it's a little bit frustrating, too, because a program recently launched,

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:21.520
and this is one I was hacking a couple days ago.

00:39:22.400 --> 00:39:26.560
And, you know, it's new scope.

00:39:26.560 --> 00:39:28.480
And so it was just a bloodbath, right?

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:29.680
There were worms everywhere.

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:36.640
And I got, besides one crit and one high, I got all dupes.

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:41.120
And the reason for that was I'm slow to find the bugs.

00:39:41.120 --> 00:39:43.440
I mean, it's just kind of who I am as a hacker.

00:39:43.440 --> 00:39:48.640
Like, I'm not, I don't have that sixth sense that some people tend to have,

00:39:48.640 --> 00:39:53.600
in that they go into the application and they just know right away where the bugs are, you know?

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:55.440
And sometimes it's luck.

00:39:55.440 --> 00:39:59.600
But there are some hackers that I know that really are just like,

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.800
no, I wouldn't even test that because there's not going to be bugs.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:04.880
I would test this place for bugs, and there's bugs there.

00:40:04.880 --> 00:40:06.320
And I'm like, what?

00:40:06.320 --> 00:40:09.680
And so I definitely am a little bit more methodical with my going in there,

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:12.480
reading the docs, using the application like a user.

00:40:12.480 --> 00:40:15.920
But a lot of times that ends up, especially when there's no dupe window,

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:20.800
with me getting some dupes on the more lower hanging fruits.

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:22.240
But I still got the crit and the high,

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:25.200
which came from a little bit more in-depth application analysis.

00:40:25.840 --> 00:40:30.160
Yeah, and so when you want to spend so much time on a program that you choose,

00:40:30.720 --> 00:40:35.840
it must be a really thought out decision to hack on the program.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:38.960
So what are the factors that you consider when choosing your program?

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:41.600
Yeah, normally it's a live hacking event invite.

00:40:43.600 --> 00:40:45.920
Yeah, to be frank, the past year it's been that.

00:40:46.800 --> 00:40:50.640
But besides that, I really enjoy the Instacart program.

00:40:50.640 --> 00:40:51.520
We might have to bleep that.

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:53.200
I don't remember if they're public or not.

00:40:53.600 --> 00:40:54.100
OK.

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:59.200
And that program is great.

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:00.480
I've enjoyed working with them.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:02.400
And the bounties are where they should be.

00:41:02.400 --> 00:41:06.560
And I'm a little bit more acquainted with the application, which is nice.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:09.920
And then also, I do look at the challenges.

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:14.720
And I do look at programs that are just launching.

00:41:14.720 --> 00:41:18.160
And if the bounties are enticing, then I'll go for those.

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:29.760
And what that looks like for me is if the mediums are above $750.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.480
So ideally, I would like them at like $1,000.

00:41:33.440 --> 00:41:37.280
Some programs have mediums up to $3,000, which is really, really nice.

00:41:38.320 --> 00:41:44.080
And then if the crits are in the, I say above $5,000,

00:41:44.080 --> 00:41:48.240
I really would like to see them above $7,500 now.

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:57.680
If the crit is below $7,500, it feels a little bit not as rewarding.

00:41:57.680 --> 00:42:01.360
Because you can go to companies like PayPal and get a medium,

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:02.720
and it's like $8,000.

00:42:03.680 --> 00:42:06.960
And so that's kind of what I look for.

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:13.120
And do you spend more time on private or public bug bounty programs?

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:13.840
Definitely private.

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:15.760
Private.

00:42:15.760 --> 00:42:17.360
And do you sort of...

00:42:18.160 --> 00:42:23.520
So when an invite comes to your inbox, and you evaluate the program,

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:26.240
what are the metrics on, let's say, HackerOne that you look at?

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:28.000
Do you look at the response time?

00:42:28.000 --> 00:42:29.120
Do you look at the leaderboard?

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:30.500
Yeah.

00:42:31.120 --> 00:42:35.840
So first of all, when I get a program invite,

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:38.000
there's a lot of program invites that are coming.

00:42:38.000 --> 00:42:40.240
So I'm not really even assessing all of them nowadays.

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:43.600
I just kind of click, accept, click, accept, click, accept.

00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:47.280
And then when I'm looking for a new program,

00:42:47.280 --> 00:42:50.720
I'm going to my opportunities tab.

00:42:50.720 --> 00:42:54.000
And sometimes I'm sorting by most recent programs,

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:55.600
and then I'll sort of read through those a little bit.

00:42:57.440 --> 00:43:01.280
And I'll look at the bounty table, like I mentioned, that sort of thing.

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:04.640
And I'll look at the response statistics.

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:10.400
Now, my response statistics criterion has changed a little bit over the years.

00:43:11.600 --> 00:43:15.120
I used to like to see sub two-week response time.

00:43:15.120 --> 00:43:16.880
And I still do, of course.

00:43:16.880 --> 00:43:22.160
But my standard has moved a little bit more lax, too.

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:25.920
If they can get me the bounty within a month, I'm pretty happy with that.

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:30.960
If there was a program that would pay, you know, in two weeks,

00:43:30.960 --> 00:43:33.040
then I think they would get a lot more engagement.

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:35.760
But times are changing,

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:38.640
and people seem to be a little bit understaffed in their security departments.

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:43.360
So some of my favorite programs that had a more traditionally fast response time

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:45.040
aren't keeping it up nowadays.

00:43:46.880 --> 00:43:51.120
Okay, and do you have some programs that you come back over and over again?

00:43:51.680 --> 00:43:53.440
Yeah, Instacart's the one, man.

00:43:53.440 --> 00:43:57.280
That's the one that I kind of go and work on.

00:43:58.240 --> 00:44:04.160
Besides that, there are a couple companies that have been running challenges iteratively.

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:07.760
You know, they run challenges on a pretty regular basis.

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:10.800
Challenges being, they'll launch new scope.

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:15.520
They'll launch a new product into scope and run a specific program for that

00:44:16.480 --> 00:44:18.720
that is capped at a specific amount of money.

00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.120
So, you know, they'll say, okay, we've got this challenge.

00:44:21.120 --> 00:44:21.840
It's new scope.

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:23.680
It's capped at 25 grand or whatever.

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:28.560
So I'll normally try to participate in those

00:44:28.560 --> 00:44:30.560
if they're one of the couple ones that I really like.

00:44:31.680 --> 00:44:35.920
But most of the time for me, it's live hacking event targets or Instacart.

00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:41.600
Okay, and let's say you come back to a program after a year.

00:44:42.480 --> 00:44:49.520
Do you sort of start from the beginning, like you never hacked here before?

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:51.840
Or do you have something that you track changes?

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:53.200
Do you check release notes?

00:44:54.160 --> 00:44:59.600
Yeah, I definitely check release notes on my targets that I am aware of.

00:45:00.480 --> 00:45:01.840
And that's always really helpful.

00:45:02.480 --> 00:45:06.320
I'm also doing this thing, and I don't think the script's running nowadays,

00:45:06.320 --> 00:45:07.840
but for like one or two targets.

00:45:07.840 --> 00:45:14.160
But I will be picking up this thing again, where I, when I'm hacking on a target,

00:45:15.040 --> 00:45:18.640
and I did that sort of JS parsing, like I mentioned before of like,

00:45:18.640 --> 00:45:22.960
all right, this is how endpoints are defined in the JavaScript files on this target, right?

00:45:24.240 --> 00:45:30.480
I will set up an automation script to go to the HTML page

00:45:30.480 --> 00:45:35.520
that contains the dynamically generated JS file, extract the JS file name,

00:45:36.240 --> 00:45:40.160
open up the JS file, and then run a set of regexes on it.

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:43.440
And extract all the endpoints out.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:44.640
And then yeah, that's so smart.

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:49.520
Diff that with an old version of that same data.

00:45:49.520 --> 00:45:51.360
And then alert if anything new pops up.

00:45:51.360 --> 00:45:57.520
So I'm getting alerted to new endpoints, new, you know, GraphQL queries and mutations.

00:45:57.520 --> 00:46:02.000
I'm getting alerts for sometimes even feature flags on the application,

00:46:02.000 --> 00:46:02.880
you know, that sort of thing.

00:46:03.920 --> 00:46:08.960
So those will allow me to be a little bit more reactive in my hacking

00:46:08.960 --> 00:46:11.600
and go after some of those new features right as they come out.

00:46:12.880 --> 00:46:13.760
Yeah, that's so good.

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:18.160
Like now I think about it is the best approach to dishing JS files that I've heard about.

00:46:18.960 --> 00:46:23.280
Recently, there was a guy that found a 2Hacker one,

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:27.840
a report 2Hacker one about an IDOR in an unreleased feature.

00:46:28.560 --> 00:46:31.760
And he did maybe not similar thing,

00:46:31.760 --> 00:46:35.920
but he just extracted words from JS files and diffed them.

00:46:36.560 --> 00:46:38.160
But I think with your approach,

00:46:38.160 --> 00:46:42.800
with actually having it sort of tailored regex to a specific application,

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:47.520
I think that's the most accurate approach to it that I've seen.

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:53.360
Yeah, and I'll give some tips to people that are thinking about implementing the same thing.

00:46:53.360 --> 00:46:57.040
I don't know that I'll ever make my version of this open sourced.

00:46:57.680 --> 00:47:00.000
But if anybody is thinking about making the same thing,

00:47:00.560 --> 00:47:06.560
I would advise you to not try to make a one size fits all system

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:10.160
where you just like input a bunch of variables and then it does it.

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:13.040
I would advise you to build a library

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:18.160
that allows you to have an easier time building these scripts,

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:21.280
but to build an individual script for every target.

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:27.120
Because every target is going to have a different structure, a different flow.

00:47:27.120 --> 00:47:30.000
Are you logging in, automating a login,

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:35.760
and then going and grabbing a HTML file that has a JS file in it?

00:47:35.760 --> 00:47:38.640
Or are you doing that same thing, but when you grab the JS file,

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:41.920
then you're extracting parts of the JS file and then you're loading it in?

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:46.880
Extracting parts of the JS file and then building more JS files and going getting in those?

00:47:46.880 --> 00:47:52.160
You know, there's lots of different iterations for you to do a very thorough analysis of

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:55.680
the JS files that the company is producing.

00:47:55.680 --> 00:48:04.240
And I think your best bet is actually just to write custom code for each and every one

00:48:04.240 --> 00:48:08.240
and have some tools in your toolkit that will allow you to do that effectively.

00:48:08.720 --> 00:48:14.320
Yeah, and from the technical side, do you just run this script on your VPS or something?

00:48:14.320 --> 00:48:14.880
Yeah, I...

00:48:14.880 --> 00:48:16.800
And you use maybe Slack notifications?

00:48:16.800 --> 00:48:23.280
Yeah, exactly. I use a webhook and I just have it in CronTab on my VPS

00:48:23.280 --> 00:48:28.320
and it just runs every, you know, five minutes or ten minutes or an hour, whatever I decide.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:35.360
And yeah, and the other cool thing about that is you get to know the company's release cycle too.

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:40.640
So you'll see like, hey, I'm getting notifications about this every Thursday at,

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:42.720
you know, 6 p.m. or something like that.

00:48:42.720 --> 00:48:47.440
So I bet they're pushing every Thursday at 6 p.m., you know, and so that's pretty cool as well.

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:52.080
Yeah, interesting. How often do you run it? Do you run it every hour?

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:55.520
Yeah, every hour, sometimes every five to ten minutes.

00:48:55.520 --> 00:48:59.760
But that's kind of overkill. I would recommend doing it every hour.

00:49:00.640 --> 00:49:05.680
Okay, but do you think if someone does it every day, it's too... it's not enough?

00:49:07.280 --> 00:49:11.920
No, not necessarily. You know, I just like to catch it as soon as I can catch it, right?

00:49:11.920 --> 00:49:17.840
Because it's a competitive game, you know, we're looking to, you know,

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:20.720
we're looking to beat other hackers to this scope.

00:49:20.720 --> 00:49:26.960
And who knows, the other hacker could just be, you know, wandering onto that program that day.

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:32.640
And but you got to react quickly if you want to be first to some of these things, so.

00:49:34.080 --> 00:49:38.160
Okay. Is it the only automation that you run these days?

00:49:38.960 --> 00:49:42.400
Yeah, that's pretty much it, man. Everything else is manual.

00:49:42.400 --> 00:49:48.720
I, just for, yeah, just for a little bit of history,

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:53.440
I was a competitor in the Recon game for many years.

00:49:54.400 --> 00:50:00.240
You know, I had my full setup with my, you know, pulling from passive DNS sources,

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:07.120
doing certs, you know, TLS certs, and, you know, brute forcing stuff.

00:50:07.120 --> 00:50:12.320
And then, you know, doing subdomain takeovers and scanning mass for vulnerabilities.

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:15.520
But for me, it got too much of a grind.

00:50:15.520 --> 00:50:22.080
And I discovered that I was spending more time writing code than I was actually hacking stuff.

00:50:22.080 --> 00:50:23.520
And I didn't like that very much.

00:50:23.520 --> 00:50:29.200
So when my bread and butter vulnerability type, the Route 53 takeovers,

00:50:29.200 --> 00:50:35.040
had a big sort of structural change that made them more tricky to implement.

00:50:35.040 --> 00:50:37.680
They're still possible, I believe, but definitely trickier to implement.

00:50:39.200 --> 00:50:42.400
Then I said, all right, let me try to pivot a little bit.

00:50:42.400 --> 00:50:46.400
And that's when I started doing a little bit more intentional manual hacking

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:50.640
and looking for more impactful vulnerabilities.

00:50:50.640 --> 00:50:54.800
But I will say lately, I've been kind of thinking about flipping back to the other side,

00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:57.680
for that same reason that we talked about, Greg, which is that

00:50:59.760 --> 00:51:03.360
if you stop working, nothing's happening, right?

00:51:04.080 --> 00:51:08.560
And I was always comforted by the fact that when I wasn't working,

00:51:08.560 --> 00:51:11.440
when I had my, you know, automation setup running,

00:51:11.440 --> 00:51:13.680
that something was always searching for vulnerabilities.

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:15.440
And sometimes I'd get that ping on my phone, like,

00:51:15.440 --> 00:51:18.960
oh, subdomain takeover, you know, great, you know.

00:51:18.960 --> 00:51:20.880
And that was a little bit comforting.

00:51:20.880 --> 00:51:26.560
So I think, you know, maybe expanding this JS file monitoring that I'm doing

00:51:27.200 --> 00:51:31.360
to more programs or getting back into the automation game just a little bit,

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:33.520
scanning for a couple of different types of vulns

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:35.840
might help me sort of relieve that stress a little bit.

00:51:37.200 --> 00:51:41.040
Yeah, I sometimes think about it when I'm talking with somebody about the career

00:51:41.040 --> 00:51:44.960
and bug bounty hunting and doing the YouTube stuff and blah, blah, blah.

00:51:44.960 --> 00:51:48.320
And sometimes we delve into the passive income discussion.

00:51:48.960 --> 00:51:50.560
And when I say, you know, when you're hacking,

00:51:50.560 --> 00:51:53.840
you have to be in front of the computer and then,

00:51:53.840 --> 00:51:57.040
yeah, I should probably exclude the automation guys from this

00:51:57.040 --> 00:51:59.200
because they can actually make money passively.

00:51:59.200 --> 00:52:01.040
Yeah, man, it's pretty awesome.

00:52:01.760 --> 00:52:04.720
And that was the fun part of all that.

00:52:04.720 --> 00:52:08.400
So it's great for those hackers that really enjoy writing code, for sure.

00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:13.440
Yeah, let's come back to live hacking events.

00:52:14.000 --> 00:52:19.040
You said that this year was mostly oriented around those hacking events.

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:24.240
So for those of us who haven't attended live hacking events,

00:52:24.240 --> 00:52:25.920
how does this cycle look like?

00:52:26.640 --> 00:52:32.160
Yeah, well, to be honest, the flow of the live hacking events,

00:52:32.160 --> 00:52:35.120
like when they occur and where they're going to be and stuff like that,

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:40.000
we, you know, sometimes we know in advance,

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:43.440
but most of the time we just know when HackerOne announces it,

00:52:43.440 --> 00:52:47.760
like, you know, on their Twitter or by sending us invites to our emails, right?

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:52.320
And so not a lot of planning in advance on that.

00:52:52.320 --> 00:52:54.640
And that's feedback I've given to HackerOne being like,

00:52:54.640 --> 00:52:58.080
hey, especially if you want me to come to every event in a year,

00:52:58.080 --> 00:52:59.920
you know, five or six events in a year,

00:53:01.520 --> 00:53:04.160
I need to know more in advance so I can plan my life a little bit.

00:53:05.520 --> 00:53:09.440
But yeah, so the typical flow is you get an email saying,

00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:12.800
hey, you're invited to the event, here's the details.

00:53:12.800 --> 00:53:14.320
Sometimes they'll tell you the target then

00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:16.480
or they'll release the target at a later date.

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:19.920
It's going to be in this place.

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:23.920
You're covered for your flight and hotel

00:53:23.920 --> 00:53:25.680
or you're not covered for your flight and hotel,

00:53:25.680 --> 00:53:27.920
depending on whether you're a plus one

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:30.000
or whether you're sort of a sponsored hacker.

00:53:31.120 --> 00:53:36.720
And then probably about two weeks before the live hacking event swings around,

00:53:36.720 --> 00:53:38.400
you get on a scope call.

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:41.920
They announce the scope, they tell you about the bonuses

00:53:41.920 --> 00:53:45.920
and the cool, you know, bounty tables and challenges

00:53:45.920 --> 00:53:47.440
and that sort of thing to have planned for the event.

00:53:48.560 --> 00:53:52.560
Is this called the first time when you see what other hackers are invited?

00:53:53.680 --> 00:53:55.120
Yeah, sort of.

00:53:55.120 --> 00:53:58.720
But, you know, it gets around in the community, you know.

00:53:58.720 --> 00:53:59.600
Yeah, I guess.

00:53:59.600 --> 00:54:00.240
You kind of know.

00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:05.840
And there are, you know, people like me and Joel

00:54:05.840 --> 00:54:11.920
that have gone to like every event since like, you know, 2018 or something like that.

00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:16.240
So, you know, there's definitely some regulars.

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:20.000
But yeah, then you have the scope call,

00:54:20.000 --> 00:54:22.480
you kind of say hey to all your friends

00:54:22.480 --> 00:54:24.800
and you kind of decide, hey, am I going to collab

00:54:24.800 --> 00:54:26.000
or am I not going to collab?

00:54:26.720 --> 00:54:30.160
And then right after the scope call, the scope opens normally.

00:54:30.160 --> 00:54:33.680
So, you know, you get the invite and you start hacking.

00:54:33.680 --> 00:54:35.680
Then it's two weeks of hardcore hacking.

00:54:36.320 --> 00:54:39.840
And this is what's called, this phase is what's called the dupe window.

00:54:39.840 --> 00:54:46.080
So, if anybody finds a bug that is the same as another bug during this period,

00:54:46.080 --> 00:54:47.440
the bounty is split in half.

00:54:49.360 --> 00:54:55.360
And so, you know, if I find a bug and you find the same bug during that period

00:54:55.360 --> 00:54:58.880
and that bug is awarded a 2k bounty, then we'll each get $1,000.

00:54:59.600 --> 00:55:02.480
But here's a little bit of catch to that, right?

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:05.920
Say me and Joel are collaborating and you do it solo, right?

00:55:06.480 --> 00:55:08.960
That bug gets split in two.

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:11.680
So, you get $1,000 and then we get $1,000,

00:55:11.680 --> 00:55:14.320
which then gets split to $500, right?

00:55:14.320 --> 00:55:18.640
So, there's lots of dynamics surrounding collaboration.

00:55:19.680 --> 00:55:21.280
And we all know the power of collaboration.

00:55:21.280 --> 00:55:22.240
It definitely allows you to...

00:55:22.800 --> 00:55:24.480
Sorry, just to clarify this,

00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:28.000
if three independent hackers or four independent hackers find a bug,

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:30.160
it's split into three or four?

00:55:30.160 --> 00:55:31.200
Yes, that's correct.

00:55:31.200 --> 00:55:31.760
Yeah.

00:55:31.760 --> 00:55:33.760
Is there an upper limit for...

00:55:33.760 --> 00:55:34.320
There is not.

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:39.360
And so, I have some emails in my inbox of saying,

00:55:39.360 --> 00:55:43.440
hey, you were awarded an $8 bounty or like a $4 bounty.

00:55:43.440 --> 00:55:45.200
And I'm like, thanks, appreciate that.

00:55:47.360 --> 00:55:50.720
But yeah, so, you know, there's definitely some collaboration stuff

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:52.240
that needs to be taken into account there.

00:55:53.360 --> 00:55:56.960
And then after the dupe window is over,

00:55:56.960 --> 00:55:59.520
most of the time people, you know, you'll kind of hit up your friends.

00:55:59.520 --> 00:56:00.800
You'll be like, hey, what did you find?

00:56:00.800 --> 00:56:02.640
And they'll be like, hey, what did you find?

00:56:02.640 --> 00:56:04.080
And you kind of share cool bugs.

00:56:04.080 --> 00:56:07.760
And then collaboration opens up a lot more at that point, I think,

00:56:07.760 --> 00:56:10.080
because people have done their initial pass

00:56:10.080 --> 00:56:11.600
and they're looking to escalate things

00:56:11.600 --> 00:56:13.760
and they're looking to see the things they missed.

00:56:13.760 --> 00:56:16.320
And then, you know, you go to the event,

00:56:17.120 --> 00:56:19.520
you collab, the bounties start rolling.

00:56:19.520 --> 00:56:24.320
And then the award ceremony happens where, you know, an MVH is decided.

00:56:25.200 --> 00:56:26.880
The MVH is decided.

00:56:26.880 --> 00:56:30.080
And it's a very exciting time for sure.

00:56:31.760 --> 00:56:34.640
Do you usually work alone at live hacking events?

00:56:34.640 --> 00:56:35.600
Do you collaborate?

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:37.040
Are you part of a bigger team?

00:56:38.480 --> 00:56:39.120
Yeah, man.

00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:43.520
So I catch a lot of flack for this, actually, but I don't normally collab.

00:56:43.520 --> 00:56:46.320
The only exception to that is when I have,

00:56:47.360 --> 00:56:51.600
when Joel decides to take time off of work and do it full time with me,

00:56:51.600 --> 00:56:53.600
then I'll do a 50-50 split with Joel,

00:56:53.600 --> 00:56:57.920
because I know him and I know what kind of hacker he is.

00:56:57.920 --> 00:56:59.680
And I think we complement each other well.

00:57:00.880 --> 00:57:05.200
But typically for me, I'm going solo until the end of the dupe period.

00:57:05.200 --> 00:57:08.080
And the reason for that is I think I have the ability

00:57:08.080 --> 00:57:13.280
to find a good amount of bugs solo.

00:57:13.280 --> 00:57:15.920
And like I mentioned before, if you find a bug on a team,

00:57:16.800 --> 00:57:18.480
not only are, and that bug is duped,

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:20.800
not only are you getting the dupe split,

00:57:20.800 --> 00:57:22.800
but you're also getting the collab split, right?

00:57:22.800 --> 00:57:24.560
So the bounties get a lot lower.

00:57:24.560 --> 00:57:28.960
So what I prefer to do is I prefer to work solo until the end of the dupe period.

00:57:29.440 --> 00:57:33.200
And then at the end of the dupe period, I'll collab with other hackers.

00:57:33.200 --> 00:57:35.760
And then at that point, if we're finding bugs that are unique,

00:57:36.400 --> 00:57:40.000
then we can submit those together

00:57:40.000 --> 00:57:43.280
and then just get the full bounty split

00:57:43.280 --> 00:57:46.080
between however many of us participated in it.

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:50.000
And that's been a good system for me over the past couple of years.

00:57:50.000 --> 00:57:52.240
And I think it maximizes bounty returns

00:57:52.240 --> 00:57:54.240
and still allows me to see a lot of cool bugs

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:55.760
and collaborate with a lot of great hackers.

00:57:56.640 --> 00:58:01.520
Do you think those dupe windows could work outside live hacking events?

00:58:01.520 --> 00:58:02.720
Frick yes, I do.

00:58:02.720 --> 00:58:05.600
I'm so salty right now because of all those dupes I got

00:58:05.600 --> 00:58:08.560
in that program that I was telling you about when a program launches.

00:58:08.560 --> 00:58:13.840
I would love to see HackerOne implement dupe windows in challenges

00:58:13.840 --> 00:58:18.800
and in, you know, just when programs launch,

00:58:18.800 --> 00:58:20.640
because it motivates hackers a lot more.

00:58:20.640 --> 00:58:22.880
For example, the target that I'm talking about,

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:26.640
it launched in the middle of the night for me.

00:58:26.640 --> 00:58:31.280
And so I missed a lot of the, you know,

00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:33.840
initial hacking hours on that program.

00:58:33.840 --> 00:58:37.280
And I like to think that's why I got, you know,

00:58:37.280 --> 00:58:38.880
more dupes than my fair share there.

00:58:40.480 --> 00:58:45.120
But yeah, I would definitely like to see that be used in other contexts as well.

00:58:46.800 --> 00:58:50.800
What are some other things or features in bug bounty platforms

00:58:50.800 --> 00:58:51.600
that you'd like to see?

00:58:52.400 --> 00:58:53.280
That's a good question.

00:58:54.320 --> 00:58:59.360
Well, I'm lucky enough to have had the opportunity to collab directly with

00:59:00.320 --> 00:59:03.840
designers for HackerOne and for other platforms as well.

00:59:05.600 --> 00:59:08.400
And give direct feedback on the stuff that I want implemented.

00:59:09.280 --> 00:59:13.120
And so most of the features that I've really been kind of yearning for

00:59:13.120 --> 00:59:15.040
have been implemented already.

00:59:18.000 --> 00:59:19.760
You know, off the top of my head,

00:59:19.760 --> 00:59:23.920
the only thing that comes to mind is a little bit more in-depth reporting

00:59:23.920 --> 00:59:25.920
and a better mobile UI,

00:59:26.560 --> 00:59:28.320
which is a complaint that I've had for a while,

00:59:28.320 --> 00:59:30.960
is that the HackerOne mobile UI is like not great.

00:59:32.160 --> 00:59:33.680
But I would definitely also...

00:59:33.680 --> 00:59:34.960
How often do you use it on mobile?

00:59:34.960 --> 00:59:35.840
I use it on mobile all the time.

00:59:35.840 --> 00:59:38.080
So like responding to reports?

00:59:38.080 --> 00:59:39.200
Yeah, responding to reports.

00:59:39.200 --> 00:59:41.520
And also just, you know, I'm out and about

00:59:41.520 --> 00:59:42.960
and I get an email saying, you know,

00:59:43.600 --> 00:59:45.840
there's some movement on this report.

00:59:45.840 --> 00:59:47.680
You know, it's like crack, man.

00:59:48.320 --> 00:59:51.200
You start shaking if you don't open the email right away, you know.

00:59:52.320 --> 00:59:54.320
So I use it on mobile a lot.

00:59:56.000 --> 00:59:56.500
Okay.

00:59:57.440 --> 00:59:59.280
Coming back to the live hacking event,

01:00:00.160 --> 01:00:05.520
imagine the situation that you lose all your bug bounty platform profiles

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:08.800
and you lose all the relationships you have,

01:00:09.360 --> 01:00:10.880
but you keep your skills.

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:14.560
What's the fastest way to get to a live hacking event?

01:00:15.280 --> 01:00:16.080
That's a good question.

01:00:17.920 --> 01:00:24.720
If I'm being honest,

01:00:25.520 --> 01:00:28.960
the fastest way to get to a live hacking event

01:00:28.960 --> 01:00:33.600
is probably to connect with other hackers

01:00:33.600 --> 01:00:35.360
that are at the live hacking events.

01:00:35.360 --> 01:00:39.040
So whether that's, you know, you try to...

01:00:40.160 --> 01:00:42.240
Well, also hackers get in...

01:00:42.800 --> 01:00:46.800
Notable hackers that are in the locality of the event

01:00:46.800 --> 01:00:47.840
also get invited.

01:00:47.840 --> 01:00:51.280
So for example, if they're having an event in Washington, D.C.,

01:00:51.280 --> 01:00:53.280
I'm an hour from Washington, D.C.

01:00:53.280 --> 01:00:55.040
So I might get an invite there,

01:00:55.040 --> 01:00:56.480
even though I'm not like, you know,

01:00:58.160 --> 01:01:00.400
the top hacker in general at that point, right?

01:01:02.160 --> 01:01:05.760
And so, you know, your locality definitely plays a role,

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:08.960
but your best chance at getting in,

01:01:08.960 --> 01:01:13.520
I believe, is probably to be a plus one in the beginning, right?

01:01:13.520 --> 01:01:16.320
And so making friends with somebody that has a plus one

01:01:16.640 --> 01:01:18.560
and asking for a plus one or that sort of thing

01:01:19.280 --> 01:01:20.640
can get you in the door.

01:01:20.640 --> 01:01:22.480
But then here's the thing,

01:01:22.480 --> 01:01:24.480
you've got to perform at that event, you know?

01:01:24.480 --> 01:01:27.280
If you don't rank top 10 at that event,

01:01:27.280 --> 01:01:28.480
unlikely you're getting back in.

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:31.840
And so that's kind of how I got into the events

01:01:31.840 --> 01:01:34.240
in the first place was I was a plus one.

01:01:34.240 --> 01:01:39.120
And then I ranked eighth in my first live hacking event.

01:01:39.120 --> 01:01:40.880
And then I got invited to the next one.

01:01:40.880 --> 01:01:43.120
And the next one, I had enough time to like fully prepare

01:01:44.240 --> 01:01:45.120
and hack.

01:01:45.120 --> 01:01:48.160
And then I've kind of continued ranking in the top 10

01:01:48.160 --> 01:01:50.080
for the next live hacking events

01:01:50.080 --> 01:01:53.840
until I won an MVH and a couple of first place trophies.

01:01:53.840 --> 01:01:56.800
And that kind of secures your place a little bit more.

01:01:58.160 --> 01:01:58.560
Yeah.

01:01:58.560 --> 01:02:01.840
Also, now I think back to sort of your hacking style

01:02:01.840 --> 01:02:04.240
that you don't report as much volume,

01:02:04.240 --> 01:02:06.400
but you go for the impact more.

01:02:06.400 --> 01:02:09.040
It's probably not the best ways

01:02:09.040 --> 01:02:12.080
to optimize for reputation points on those platforms.

01:02:12.640 --> 01:02:16.000
So yeah, now it makes sense for you.

01:02:16.000 --> 01:02:17.760
Well, let me clarify that really quickly.

01:02:17.760 --> 01:02:19.600
Like I report every bug I find.

01:02:19.600 --> 01:02:20.800
Don't get me wrong.

01:02:20.800 --> 01:02:23.680
Like every, you know, if there's a low,

01:02:23.680 --> 01:02:25.680
I'm going to report that shit all day, you know?

01:02:27.440 --> 01:02:30.240
And so it's not that I'm not looking for...

01:02:31.440 --> 01:02:32.960
I'm definitely focusing on impact.

01:02:32.960 --> 01:02:34.320
I'm trying to achieve impact.

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:38.720
But I definitely don't shy away from reporting,

01:02:38.720 --> 01:02:41.840
you know, lows or mediums or anything like that

01:02:41.920 --> 01:02:43.920
at the expense of spending more time

01:02:43.920 --> 01:02:45.760
finding highs or criticals.

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:47.600
Okay.

01:02:49.600 --> 01:02:53.760
And so in the last year, for example,

01:02:53.760 --> 01:02:57.120
how many programs did you report bugs to?

01:02:57.120 --> 01:02:58.080
Dude, very few.

01:02:58.880 --> 01:03:00.320
Let me pull up the data right here.

01:03:00.320 --> 01:03:00.880
I've got it.

01:03:02.560 --> 01:03:02.880
All right.

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:03.680
So I'm looking here.

01:03:03.680 --> 01:03:08.000
One, two, three, four, five, six, seven, eight, nine, ten.

01:03:09.280 --> 01:03:11.440
Probably barely more than ten.

01:03:12.800 --> 01:03:13.440
Yeah.

01:03:13.440 --> 01:03:15.440
Eleven is kind of where I'm at.

01:03:16.320 --> 01:03:18.080
So very few programs.

01:03:20.480 --> 01:03:24.000
And I think that's a function of me

01:03:25.040 --> 01:03:27.440
focusing specifically on live hacking event targets

01:03:27.440 --> 01:03:30.720
and also just not having a lot of time to hack this year

01:03:30.720 --> 01:03:34.960
because of working on the rental property

01:03:34.960 --> 01:03:37.680
and some other personal stuff that's come up.

01:03:38.480 --> 01:03:40.960
You know, I've pretty much only been working hard

01:03:40.960 --> 01:03:42.080
on the live hacking events

01:03:42.080 --> 01:03:45.040
and then working on other programs on the side.

01:03:46.800 --> 01:03:46.960
Yeah.

01:03:46.960 --> 01:03:52.480
And one follow-up question to the thing about networking

01:03:52.480 --> 01:03:54.320
and in the context of live hacking events.

01:03:55.120 --> 01:03:58.560
So for people that don't know anybody,

01:03:59.840 --> 01:04:02.000
they sort of probably follow your podcast.

01:04:02.000 --> 01:04:04.160
They follow my media on Twitter,

01:04:04.160 --> 01:04:06.400
but they don't know anybody personally.

01:04:06.400 --> 01:04:09.120
How you think they can build those connections,

01:04:09.120 --> 01:04:10.400
build those relationships?

01:04:10.400 --> 01:04:10.640
Yeah.

01:04:10.640 --> 01:04:13.280
I mean, the best way to build those relationships

01:04:13.280 --> 01:04:15.920
is by interacting with people in meaningful ways

01:04:15.920 --> 01:04:21.200
on Twitter and on Discord or X, excuse me.

01:04:22.080 --> 01:04:24.800
You know, the security community,

01:04:24.800 --> 01:04:26.400
particularly the bug bounty community,

01:04:26.400 --> 01:04:28.560
is pretty active on X, on Twitter.

01:04:29.440 --> 01:04:31.360
And so definitely be involved in that environment.

01:04:33.440 --> 01:04:34.480
Discord as well.

01:04:34.480 --> 01:04:37.200
I personally have a really hard time

01:04:37.200 --> 01:04:38.640
keeping up with Discord.

01:04:39.280 --> 01:04:41.520
Joel lives at Discord.

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:44.480
Joel is all over the place in Discord.

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:45.920
He's in every Discord server.

01:04:45.920 --> 01:04:47.840
He's pretty actively responding to stuff

01:04:47.840 --> 01:04:49.360
and I don't know how he does it.

01:04:50.720 --> 01:04:53.760
But for me, it's Twitter.

01:04:53.760 --> 01:04:58.160
And for example, let me give you someone

01:04:58.160 --> 01:04:59.680
to look up to about this.

01:04:59.680 --> 01:05:02.080
Rezo does a really good job of this, right?

01:05:02.080 --> 01:05:05.120
He's constantly engaging in Discord.

01:05:05.120 --> 01:05:07.120
He's constantly engaging on Twitter.

01:05:07.120 --> 01:05:09.520
He's adding meaningful stuff to the conversation.

01:05:10.880 --> 01:05:14.240
And yeah, I mean, everyone knows him.

01:05:14.240 --> 01:05:15.920
Everyone takes notice of that.

01:05:15.920 --> 01:05:18.720
And so it's definitely,

01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:21.760
especially if your goal is to get to a live hacking event,

01:05:23.200 --> 01:05:26.240
there is definitely some social aspect of it as well.

01:05:28.320 --> 01:05:28.880
Okay.

01:05:28.880 --> 01:05:32.960
And also in one of the,

01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:35.920
I think two or three episodes ago,

01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:39.680
you talked about the mentorship with two of your students.

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:43.680
What did you learn while teaching them?

01:05:43.680 --> 01:05:46.720
What were the mistakes that they did

01:05:46.720 --> 01:05:48.480
that you maybe didn't expect?

01:05:48.480 --> 01:05:49.840
Yeah, well, you know,

01:05:49.840 --> 01:05:51.280
one of the things that they talked about

01:05:53.040 --> 01:05:56.080
in that podcast was this concept of Joshikihara,

01:05:56.080 --> 01:05:58.480
which is a term that they coined in Japanese,

01:05:59.440 --> 01:06:03.040
which essentially means like common sense harassment.

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:09.200
But essentially, there's a term for it already,

01:06:09.200 --> 01:06:10.720
apparently called the curse of knowledge,

01:06:10.720 --> 01:06:12.480
which we found out afterwards.

01:06:12.480 --> 01:06:14.960
But essentially, once you've been in an environment

01:06:14.960 --> 01:06:17.440
and you've had a set of knowledge for so long,

01:06:17.440 --> 01:06:19.120
it's kind of hard for you to consider

01:06:19.120 --> 01:06:21.040
what life is like without that knowledge

01:06:21.040 --> 01:06:24.000
and that other people may not have that knowledge.

01:06:25.200 --> 01:06:28.160
And so I definitely found

01:06:28.160 --> 01:06:29.840
that I need to be a little bit more thorough

01:06:29.840 --> 01:06:30.880
with my explanations,

01:06:30.880 --> 01:06:32.720
especially since I've been doing this,

01:06:32.720 --> 01:06:34.720
you know, since I was 12 or 13.

01:06:34.720 --> 01:06:37.360
So most of my life, most of my cognizant life,

01:06:38.240 --> 01:06:39.040
I've been doing this.

01:06:39.680 --> 01:06:40.640
So it's a little bit hard for me

01:06:40.640 --> 01:06:42.880
to put myself in their shoes.

01:06:44.240 --> 01:06:47.600
I also learned the importance of the Socratic method,

01:06:47.600 --> 01:06:52.720
which is essentially teaching via asking questions

01:06:53.840 --> 01:06:55.280
and also just a variant on that,

01:06:55.280 --> 01:06:58.000
which is teaching via, you know, teaching

01:06:58.080 --> 01:07:01.600
and then having them teach it back to you.

01:07:02.160 --> 01:07:03.280
That has a lot of value.

01:07:04.320 --> 01:07:06.640
And if they can't teach it back to you correctly,

01:07:06.640 --> 01:07:07.920
then they don't fully understand it.

01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:13.520
And so that's definitely a high value principle as well.

01:07:14.960 --> 01:07:17.280
And for the audience here

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:20.320
that don't have any mentor

01:07:20.320 --> 01:07:24.400
and most of the audience don't have any mentor,

01:07:25.280 --> 01:07:27.760
don't write a DM to Justin to mentor you

01:07:27.760 --> 01:07:30.320
because he won't, that's one point.

01:07:31.120 --> 01:07:31.920
And the second is,

01:07:32.880 --> 01:07:35.680
you can also implement sort of learning

01:07:35.680 --> 01:07:36.960
by asking questions

01:07:36.960 --> 01:07:39.760
when you just publish stuff on your blog or on Twitter.

01:07:40.880 --> 01:07:43.520
Simply you can write about anything that you learn,

01:07:44.160 --> 01:07:45.360
some experience you've had,

01:07:45.360 --> 01:07:46.640
some challenge you solved,

01:07:47.200 --> 01:07:51.200
some anything that's sort of in your learning journey.

01:07:52.480 --> 01:07:55.760
Because I know it from the beginning of the channel,

01:07:55.760 --> 01:07:59.280
like the beginning of my channel was basically this,

01:07:59.280 --> 01:08:01.440
was basically, oh, I think this report is cool

01:08:01.440 --> 01:08:02.720
and I think I understand it.

01:08:03.680 --> 01:08:06.720
But when I started to actually make a video about it,

01:08:06.720 --> 01:08:09.040
I was a lot of the times realizing

01:08:09.040 --> 01:08:11.200
I did not understand it at all.

01:08:11.200 --> 01:08:13.200
It was only scratching the surface.

01:08:13.200 --> 01:08:17.920
So it's a really, really good way to actually understand

01:08:17.920 --> 01:08:20.720
because you're also prepared for other people

01:08:20.720 --> 01:08:22.640
to sort of read it.

01:08:22.640 --> 01:08:24.080
So you want to make sure

01:08:25.920 --> 01:08:29.520
that you will not be wrong here in a stupid way.

01:08:29.520 --> 01:08:31.200
So it's really, really good.

01:08:31.200 --> 01:08:33.440
Yeah, I totally agree with that.

01:08:33.440 --> 01:08:34.720
And just to echo,

01:08:34.720 --> 01:08:37.520
even if you don't understand something fully

01:08:37.520 --> 01:08:39.600
or if you can't fully exploit something

01:08:39.600 --> 01:08:41.360
or you just find some weird quirk,

01:08:41.360 --> 01:08:42.320
you can blog about that.

01:08:42.320 --> 01:08:43.280
You can write about that.

01:08:43.280 --> 01:08:44.640
You can tweet about that.

01:08:44.640 --> 01:08:46.720
And people from the community

01:08:47.440 --> 01:08:49.360
will sort of come along and be like,

01:08:49.360 --> 01:08:50.320
oh, that's interesting

01:08:50.320 --> 01:08:51.920
or nah, here's the solution for that.

01:08:51.920 --> 01:08:53.520
That's easy, that sort of thing.

01:08:53.520 --> 01:08:54.960
In either way, you learn.

01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:56.880
So that's really cool.

01:08:56.880 --> 01:08:58.480
And just going back to the mentorship note,

01:08:59.280 --> 01:09:00.960
like you pointed out, Greg,

01:09:00.960 --> 01:09:03.440
I would love to mentor all of you,

01:09:03.440 --> 01:09:05.840
but I get way too many mentorship requests

01:09:05.840 --> 01:09:09.360
and I can't even handle responding to my DMs,

01:09:09.360 --> 01:09:13.200
let alone establishing a mentor relationship with people.

01:09:13.200 --> 01:09:16.560
So I would definitely encourage people

01:09:16.560 --> 01:09:19.360
to check out the pinned tweet that I've got

01:09:19.440 --> 01:09:24.320
on how to essentially go from zero to 100K in Bug Bounty.

01:09:25.120 --> 01:09:27.600
That kind of lays out a little bit of a flow on it.

01:09:27.600 --> 01:09:31.760
And also, like you said, collaborate.

01:09:33.360 --> 01:09:34.720
Put stuff out for the community.

01:09:35.600 --> 01:09:37.280
You don't always need a mentor.

01:09:38.720 --> 01:09:40.800
Sometimes you just need somebody to motivate you

01:09:40.800 --> 01:09:42.800
to be your hacking partner.

01:09:42.800 --> 01:09:45.120
If you look at Shubbs and Nafi,

01:09:45.120 --> 01:09:48.400
the reason they both had a lot of success in the industry,

01:09:48.400 --> 01:09:51.760
both of them would credit largely to the competition

01:09:51.760 --> 01:09:53.040
between the two of them,

01:09:53.040 --> 01:09:54.480
trying to outperform each other,

01:09:54.480 --> 01:09:56.960
trying to figure out how did he do that,

01:09:56.960 --> 01:09:57.920
you know, that sort of thing.

01:09:58.800 --> 01:10:00.960
That sort of competition is really good for growth as well.

01:10:02.880 --> 01:10:08.720
Yeah, and one other thing that you mentioned in that episode

01:10:08.720 --> 01:10:11.120
was that, I think it was that episode,

01:10:11.840 --> 01:10:13.680
that full-time Bug Bounty Hunter

01:10:13.680 --> 01:10:15.840
should have a grind mentality.

01:10:15.840 --> 01:10:17.360
Can you explain what did you mean?

01:10:18.800 --> 01:10:20.000
It's a little bit tricky with that.

01:10:22.960 --> 01:10:26.480
And I guess that's largely contextually dependent.

01:10:26.480 --> 01:10:29.280
I'm not 100% sure that that's for everyone.

01:10:29.280 --> 01:10:30.800
But at the end of the day,

01:10:31.920 --> 01:10:37.040
this is not an easy job to do,

01:10:38.080 --> 01:10:39.600
being a full-time Bug Bounty Hunter.

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:42.880
And there's going to be a lot of time

01:10:42.880 --> 01:10:45.440
where you are frustrated with dupes,

01:10:45.440 --> 01:10:47.520
or you're frustrated like I am right now,

01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:50.160
or you're frustrated with, you know,

01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:51.440
how much income you made,

01:10:51.440 --> 01:10:53.760
or maybe this web application is just,

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:54.880
you know, beating you up

01:10:54.880 --> 01:10:56.800
and you can't find any bones on it,

01:10:56.800 --> 01:10:58.160
you know, and it just feels like

01:10:58.160 --> 01:10:59.280
you're getting punched in the gut.

01:11:00.960 --> 01:11:04.480
And so it's important to persevere through that

01:11:04.480 --> 01:11:06.640
and realize that that's all part of the journey.

01:11:06.640 --> 01:11:09.360
And as I'm saying this, I'm preaching to myself here

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:12.480
because, you know, it's hard to live that out.

01:11:12.480 --> 01:11:15.200
And I struggle with that today and yesterday.

01:11:15.600 --> 01:11:20.000
And oftentimes there's periods of relief from that

01:11:20.000 --> 01:11:21.760
where I'm not really thinking about it.

01:11:21.760 --> 01:11:24.320
But the times when it does happen,

01:11:25.680 --> 01:11:28.000
it's very important to rely on your friends

01:11:28.000 --> 01:11:29.040
for encouragement,

01:11:29.040 --> 01:11:31.920
rely on your, you know, significant other,

01:11:32.640 --> 01:11:34.800
rely on your religion,

01:11:34.800 --> 01:11:36.400
for me, my relationship with Jesus,

01:11:37.280 --> 01:11:41.200
to get that extra boost you need to kind of push through.

01:11:42.160 --> 01:11:47.200
Yeah, and you also mentioned your wife, Mariah.

01:11:47.200 --> 01:11:48.000
Yep, that's right.

01:11:48.000 --> 01:11:49.840
You spell it a bit differently than we do.

01:11:49.840 --> 01:11:51.120
We have the same name in foreign,

01:11:51.120 --> 01:11:52.000
but we spell it differently.

01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:52.800
That's why I sort of...

01:11:52.800 --> 01:11:55.760
Yeah, yeah, it's M-A-R-I-A-H.

01:11:55.760 --> 01:11:58.400
And, but almost everyone writes it Maria,

01:11:59.600 --> 01:12:00.800
you know, without the H at the end.

01:12:00.800 --> 01:12:02.160
So she responds to both.

01:12:03.520 --> 01:12:06.320
Okay, but my question is,

01:12:06.320 --> 01:12:09.600
how can a partner of a bug bounty hunter

01:12:10.160 --> 01:12:12.640
support a bug bounty hunter?

01:12:12.640 --> 01:12:15.280
And the intention of this question is

01:12:15.280 --> 01:12:17.040
for bug bounty hunters out there

01:12:17.840 --> 01:12:19.680
to show it to their partners.

01:12:19.680 --> 01:12:23.200
Well, I should probably have Mariah over here

01:12:23.200 --> 01:12:23.920
to talk about it.

01:12:24.880 --> 01:12:27.600
And I think I will do a podcast episode with her sometimes

01:12:27.600 --> 01:12:29.040
just kind of talk about this topic.

01:12:29.040 --> 01:12:34.800
But I think Mariah is a very data-driven person

01:12:35.440 --> 01:12:40.160
and a very practical person.

01:12:40.160 --> 01:12:42.160
And so when she looks at the data and she says,

01:12:42.160 --> 01:12:44.800
hey, Justin, you've been making more money

01:12:44.800 --> 01:12:46.480
from bug bounty doing part-time

01:12:46.480 --> 01:12:49.040
than you have been, you know, full-time bug bounty

01:12:49.040 --> 01:12:50.160
or full-time job,

01:12:51.200 --> 01:12:52.720
you should definitely go full-time bug bounty.

01:12:52.720 --> 01:12:55.200
And that's just a very obvious choice for her.

01:12:55.200 --> 01:12:57.200
And she's very confident in that decision.

01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:58.640
And so that helps reassure me.

01:12:59.520 --> 01:13:01.440
So her confidence in that sort of thing

01:13:01.440 --> 01:13:03.120
and her confidence in the fact that

01:13:03.840 --> 01:13:07.600
bug bounty is not a skill that you just lose,

01:13:08.320 --> 01:13:11.760
which is somehow something that doesn't get into our heads

01:13:11.760 --> 01:13:13.440
as hackers, you know?

01:13:13.440 --> 01:13:15.280
I've been doing this five years.

01:13:15.280 --> 01:13:17.440
And when I take a three-month break or whatever,

01:13:17.440 --> 01:13:19.120
or two-month break or something like that,

01:13:19.120 --> 01:13:21.120
and I come back, I feel rusty as heck.

01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:22.880
And I'm like, man, that's it.

01:13:22.880 --> 01:13:23.840
I don't got it anymore.

01:13:23.840 --> 01:13:25.840
Like, you know, this is the end, you know?

01:13:26.720 --> 01:13:29.600
And Mariah's like, you are so silly right now.

01:13:29.600 --> 01:13:32.160
Like, just go hack stuff for a week.

01:13:32.160 --> 01:13:32.720
You'll be fine.

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:36.240
And she's very encouraging in that regard.

01:13:36.240 --> 01:13:38.080
And then also just practical stuff as well.

01:13:38.080 --> 01:13:40.400
Like, you know, when I'm hacking,

01:13:42.240 --> 01:13:43.680
she knows that I'm in the zone.

01:13:43.680 --> 01:13:46.800
So I'm not as emotional when I respond,

01:13:46.800 --> 01:13:48.480
or emotive in any way, you know?

01:13:48.480 --> 01:13:49.840
Sometimes I talk like a robot,

01:13:49.840 --> 01:13:52.880
and she understands that that's just because I'm in the zone.

01:13:52.880 --> 01:13:54.560
And sometimes I forget to eat,

01:13:54.560 --> 01:13:56.560
and she'll bring me, you know, healthy food.

01:13:56.560 --> 01:13:58.800
And sometimes I forget to drink water,

01:13:58.800 --> 01:13:59.920
and she'll bring me water.

01:13:59.920 --> 01:14:02.400
And so, you know, those sort of things

01:14:02.400 --> 01:14:04.720
really mean a lot to me.

01:14:04.720 --> 01:14:08.960
They really show love very strongly.

01:14:08.960 --> 01:14:12.000
And I don't know how I would do it without her.

01:14:12.000 --> 01:14:12.500
So.

01:14:13.920 --> 01:14:17.760
Yeah, so I'd really love to listen to an episode of her show.

01:14:18.320 --> 01:14:19.360
It was a great idea.

01:14:19.360 --> 01:14:23.520
And now let's go to the next part,

01:14:23.520 --> 01:14:27.360
where let's talk about critical thinking.

01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:28.400
And the first question is,

01:14:28.960 --> 01:14:30.640
why to share your knowledge

01:14:30.640 --> 01:14:32.640
when you could just keep it for yourself?

01:14:32.640 --> 01:14:34.080
Yeah, that's a great question, man.

01:14:34.960 --> 01:14:37.440
My dad asks me this every single time I see him,

01:14:37.440 --> 01:14:38.560
because he's like,

01:14:38.560 --> 01:14:40.400
don't give away the hacking secrets.

01:14:40.400 --> 01:14:41.760
Like, this is your secret sauce.

01:14:41.760 --> 01:14:42.880
Don't share it, you know?

01:14:43.840 --> 01:14:44.880
And I understand what he's saying

01:14:44.880 --> 01:14:46.240
from like the perspective of,

01:14:48.160 --> 01:14:49.840
you know, trying to protect my income

01:14:49.840 --> 01:14:51.440
and not sharing it with competitors

01:14:51.440 --> 01:14:52.160
and stuff like that.

01:14:52.160 --> 01:14:53.200
But at the end of the day,

01:14:54.160 --> 01:14:59.120
the only reason I learned to hack as well as I have

01:14:59.120 --> 01:15:00.640
is because people have shared stuff.

01:15:01.520 --> 01:15:03.920
It would have been very hard for me to go from zero to,

01:15:05.040 --> 01:15:07.760
this is, you know, now I understand how to hack things.

01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:11.840
So one of it, a part of it is giving back to the community.

01:15:11.840 --> 01:15:15.200
But another piece of it is just that

01:15:15.840 --> 01:15:17.600
I want other people to succeed.

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:20.160
And it's one of my greatest joys in life

01:15:20.160 --> 01:15:24.720
to see, you know, somebody who's learning bug bounty,

01:15:26.640 --> 01:15:29.680
sort of grasp a topic and become very excited about it.

01:15:29.680 --> 01:15:32.160
And I wish I could do that individually

01:15:32.160 --> 01:15:33.600
with each and every person.

01:15:33.600 --> 01:15:35.200
You know, like you said,

01:15:35.200 --> 01:15:37.600
the people reaching out to me to get mentored.

01:15:38.160 --> 01:15:40.000
I would love to mentor each and every one of you.

01:15:40.000 --> 01:15:41.840
If I had the time, I would do it.

01:15:42.800 --> 01:15:44.320
Because it brings me so much joy

01:15:44.320 --> 01:15:47.520
to see ideas click and vulnerabilities found.

01:15:48.080 --> 01:15:49.680
But I don't.

01:15:49.680 --> 01:15:54.720
And so I figured I would do this more at scale via a podcast.

01:15:55.440 --> 01:15:58.320
And yeah, I think there's also something

01:15:58.320 --> 01:16:00.160
to just be said for having an audience.

01:16:01.440 --> 01:16:06.240
That's very helpful for, I guess,

01:16:06.240 --> 01:16:08.720
being respected in the industry.

01:16:08.720 --> 01:16:10.080
And it's very helpful for

01:16:10.960 --> 01:16:13.280
if I wanted to start any businesses in the future,

01:16:13.280 --> 01:16:15.120
launching it from having an audience

01:16:15.120 --> 01:16:17.520
is way better than launching it from not having an audience.

01:16:18.080 --> 01:16:21.040
So, and then the final thing is,

01:16:23.280 --> 01:16:25.600
I always wanted a bug bounty podcast

01:16:25.600 --> 01:16:28.480
that brings sort of the conversation,

01:16:28.480 --> 01:16:31.200
the awesome conversations and connections

01:16:31.200 --> 01:16:33.680
you have with other hackers at the live hacking events

01:16:33.680 --> 01:16:37.360
that brings that, you know, to our every single week.

01:16:37.360 --> 01:16:38.880
And so that's kind of what we're shooting for

01:16:38.880 --> 01:16:40.400
with the podcast is like,

01:16:40.400 --> 01:16:42.880
let's talk about this stuff at a very high level,

01:16:43.680 --> 01:16:47.280
at a level where, you know,

01:16:47.280 --> 01:16:48.480
if you're not at that level,

01:16:48.480 --> 01:16:52.080
then you have a good picture of what it looks like.

01:16:52.080 --> 01:16:53.280
And if you are at that level,

01:16:53.280 --> 01:16:55.600
then this is intellectually engaging for you.

01:16:57.360 --> 01:17:01.040
And you can enjoy that from an intellectual perspective.

01:17:01.040 --> 01:17:02.720
So that's kind of what we're shooting for.

01:17:04.400 --> 01:17:05.120
Yeah, that's great.

01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:06.480
Where does the name come from?

01:17:06.480 --> 01:17:08.400
The Critical Thinking Bug Bounty Podcast?

01:17:08.400 --> 01:17:09.360
Yeah, it's a little bit,

01:17:10.240 --> 01:17:12.560
the name is a little bit weird, man,

01:17:12.560 --> 01:17:15.760
because like, from an SEO perspective,

01:17:15.760 --> 01:17:18.800
like very hard to rank for critical thinking, right?

01:17:20.640 --> 01:17:23.040
But the name was actually generated

01:17:23.040 --> 01:17:25.520
by the early versions of ChatGPT.

01:17:27.280 --> 01:17:28.800
And essentially what I said was like,

01:17:28.800 --> 01:17:30.560
hey, we're gonna do a bug bounty podcast.

01:17:31.600 --> 01:17:34.480
We'd like, you know, some cool,

01:17:35.200 --> 01:17:37.200
engaging sort of punny name maybe.

01:17:37.760 --> 01:17:39.200
And we gave it a bunch of terms

01:17:39.200 --> 01:17:40.960
that are related to the industry.

01:17:40.960 --> 01:17:42.640
And it came up with critical thinking.

01:17:43.200 --> 01:17:44.240
And I was like,

01:17:44.240 --> 01:17:46.720
the other one that was kind of in our,

01:17:48.320 --> 01:17:49.760
there was an option was like,

01:17:50.320 --> 01:17:53.360
I think it was like bounty bites or something like that.

01:17:53.360 --> 01:17:56.000
Or maybe it's like, yeah, I believe that was it.

01:17:56.000 --> 01:17:57.280
But that sounded too short.

01:17:57.280 --> 01:17:58.640
You know, we were gonna do an hour,

01:17:58.640 --> 01:18:00.000
hour and a half long podcast.

